openai/gpt-oss-120b,Female,Associate's degree,Conservative,31,1,"Expanding nuclear power gives us reliable, low‑cost electricity and cuts our reliance on foreign energy sources, while the safety record is solid and it isn’t hampered by endless environmental red tape.",4,"Scientific investment is advocated to ensure that the transition away from fossil fuels is handled responsibly, avoiding the creation of a toxic legacy for future generations through improved safety and disposal standards.","The safety record of modern reactors is already solid, so spending years on extra research and “perfect” disposal standards just stalls the projects that will cut our reliance on foreign oil; we need to build the plants now, not wait for an ideal‑world study that never ends.","Investment in nuclear expansion is perceived as a diversion of critical resources away from more rapidly deployable, cost-effective, and safer renewable energy and storage technologies.","While renewables look good on paper, they still need massive, pricey storage and grid upgrades before they can truly replace baseload power—something nuclear provides today at a lower cost and without the endless permitting delays, so investing in reactors isn’t a diversion, it’s the backbone that lets any green tech actually work."
gemini-3-flash-preview,Male,High school graduate,Very conservative,14,1,"We need to stop messing around with unreliable energy sources and rapidly build more nuclear plants to ensure we have cheap, dependable power for our country. It's about achieving true energy independence and supporting our economy without being held back by unnecessary environmental regulations.",4,The development of more efficient and sustainable nuclear implementations is favored to maximize the benefits of clean energy while minimizing the risks of accidents and environmental degradation.,"Waiting for some ""perfect"" or sustainable version is just another way to delay our energy independence with more government red tape and ""green"" buzzwords. We need to stop worrying about every minor environmental risk and start building proven, reliable plants right now to get our economy back on track.","High upfront capital costs, frequent construction delays, and massive long-term liabilities are seen as significant financial burdens on taxpayers and ratepayers that may outweigh potential economic gains.","Those delays and high costs are mostly caused by the government's own red tape and endless regulations getting in the way of progress. If we just cut the bureaucracy and let people get to work, we’d have the reliable, cheap power this country needs to stay competitive and independent."
